sections.s2_technical:
Serialization writes the state of one object. The engine iterates the instance fields that are non-static and non-transient and writes their values. Static fields are excluded by definition because they are not part of any instance; there is exactly one static field shared by the whole class, independent of how many objects exist.

The practical consequence is a classic trap. Suppose a class has a static field holding a shared counter or a shared rate. You serialize an object while the static field is one value, then later change the static field, then deserialize. The deserialized object will see the new static value, not the old one, because the old value was never stored:

```java
class RateHolder implements java.io.Serializable {
    static java.math.BigDecimal baseRate = new java.math.BigDecimal("6.50"); // shared, NOT serialized
    String accountId;
}
// serialize while baseRate = 6.50
// change baseRate = 7.25
// deserialize → the object reads baseRate = 7.25, not 6.50
```

This is correct behavior, not a bug, but it surprises people who assume the entire object graph, including class-level data, is frozen into the bytes.

A related point is transient versus static. Both are skipped by serialization, but for different reasons: transient is an explicit per-field decision to exclude instance state, while static is excluded because it is class state, not instance state. You never need to mark a static field transient; it is already excluded.

A senior point is that this behavior affects design. If a value must survive serialization with the object, it has to be an instance field, not static. Conversely, if a value is genuinely class-wide configuration, like a shared base rate, keeping it static is correct precisely because each deserialized object should pick up the current configuration rather than a stale snapshot. Another senior point is that constants, which are static final, also do not travel in the bytes, so changing a constant and redeploying means every deserialized object reflects the new constant, which is usually exactly what you want.

